<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ivan Asen II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 1241</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ivan Asen II, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-asen-i">Ivan Asen I</a>, was tsar of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> from 1218 to 1241. In 1230 he defeated and captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodore-komnenos-doukas">Theodore Komnenos Doukas</a>, ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessalonica</a>, at Klokotnitsa, after which Bulgaria stretched from the Adriatic to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> and Thessalonica's new ruler Manuel became his client. He absorbed lands such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melnik">Melnik</a> and in 1235 restored the Bulgarian patriarchate with Nicaean agreement.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ivan-asen-ii.jpg" alt="Ivan Asen II" width="320" height="272" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CNG Coins · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coin_of_Ivan_Asen_II.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
